    "math_academy_id": "q-19532",
    "knowledge_point_id": "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square",
    "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+35 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+({6}^{2}-{6}^{2})+35 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-12x+36)-36+35 \\\\  & =(x-6)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x=10\\int \\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-6.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
